From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 04:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] timer: optionally count host ticks in nanoseconds

Under TCG the guest's TSC is cpu_get_host_ticks() plus an offset.  On
x86 hosts that is RDTSC (a few GHz); on hosts without a cycle counter,
which includes the WebAssembly build, it is get_clock(), exactly 1 GHz.
The migration stream carries the offset but not the rate, so a guest that
calibrated its TSC on a native x86 QEMU and is resumed in the browser
would see its TSC run several times too slowly (udelay(), sched_clock(),
and the clocksource until the kernel's watchdog retires it).

With -DPORTY_NS_HOST_TICKS a native build uses the same nanosecond counter
as the WebAssembly build.  Porty's snapshot producer is built that way.
Nothing changes without the define.
---
 include/qemu/timer.h | 16 +++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)
diff --git a/include/qemu/timer.h b/include/qemu/timer.h
index 9a366e5..3d77134 100644
--- a/include/qemu/timer.h
+++ b/include/qemu/timer.h
@@ -846,7 +846,21 @@ static inline int64_t get_clock(void)
 /*******************************************/
 /* host CPU ticks (if available) */
 
-#if defined(_ARCH_PPC)
+#if defined(PORTY_NS_HOST_TICKS)
+/*
+ * Porty: count host ticks in nanoseconds, as hosts without a cycle counter
+ * do (the fallback at the end of this list; that is what the WebAssembly
+ * build uses).  Under TCG the guest's TSC is this counter, so a native
+ * build made with -DPORTY_NS_HOST_TICKS gives the guest the same 1 GHz TSC
+ * as the browser build, and a guest saved by one (migration) keeps a
+ * correctly calibrated TSC when the other resumes it.
+ */
+static inline int64_t cpu_get_host_ticks(void)
+{
+    return get_clock();
+}
+
+#elif defined(_ARCH_PPC)
 
 static inline int64_t cpu_get_host_ticks(void)
 {
